A||||12||CUTTER||HELP: Jiggly steering wheel||||1||
Z||000000||CUTTER||06-05-2000||02:55 AM||||yes, my steering collum needs those 4 screws tightned and has been like this for months, doesnt seem to get worse. it moves about 1 inch every way. I have a appointment next week, and i drive around 50-75 miles a day. Is there any kind of a chance that the steering collum/wheel will fall off or break? I didnt know why it moved untill like a few weeks ago and now finally getting it fixed. Am I safe for about a week yet?<BR>Thanks, Shane||||reg||1
Z||000001||87ECO5||06-05-2000||08:33 AM||||I drove my coupe with a shaky column for six months.  It never seemed to get any worse.||||reg||1
Z||000002||ChadMan||06-05-2000||09:27 AM||||Mine has about 1/8" of deflection at the wheel rim.  It has been like that for over a year and hasn't gotten any worse since I stopped letting the tilt column bang into its stops every time I get out of the car.<P>I think this problem is more an annoyance than a safety issue.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Gold-86SE||06-05-2000||09:47 AM||||<BR>I know this will, at the least, sound ignorant, and the most sound stupid, but here it goes.<P>What four screws are you talking about?<P>The column is held up by two nuts toward the bottom part of the column, then at the top, just under the instrumnent cluster there is a bracket. This bracket is attached to the column with 4 bolts and then two screws are used to attached the column to the dash.<P>Now if it is these four bolts that are mentioned as being loose, mine were as tight as tight could be, yet my steering wheel wobbles something terrible.<P>Upon inspection of the steering wheel and column, it appears to me to be something within the column itself that is loose, which is causing my problems.<P>So, are there bolts within the column itself that need to be tightened? Someone enlighten me.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Linx||06-05-2000||10:22 AM||||mine too.<P>yknow- as often as this happens, you'd think there's be a recall note on it.  or something like that.||||reg||1
Z||000005||terryk||06-05-2000||12:37 PM||||Deleted<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 06-05-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||ChadMan||06-05-2000||12:54 PM||||The four screws (bolts?) in question are buried inside the steering column.  They can be tightened, but to do so, you must first remove the steering wheel, lock cylinder, and miscellaneous other stuff.<P>I posted about my problem a while back - the general consensus was that while your column can be repaired, it's quicker and much easier to replace it with a wobble-free column from another car.<P>Since my problem is so minor, I've decided to just live with it until I feel like fixing it!||||reg||1
Z||000007||87ECO5||06-05-2000||12:54 PM||||There are several threads out here that cover the details on repairing the column.  I'll summarize the problem by saying:<P>There are four screws <B><I>INSIDE</I></B> the column the work loose when the owner allows the column to slam against its stops after pulling on the tilt lever.<P>The way to avoid the problem is to hold the wheel when releasing the tilt and <B><I>SLOWLY</I></B> allowing the wheel to raise up and out of the way.||||reg||1
Z||000008||87ECO5||06-05-2000||12:56 PM||||WOW, ChadMan!  Check out the times on our posts!  Great minds think alike!    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000009||TOM||06-06-2000||12:45 AM||||I disagree. It takes 20 minutes to fix the column. I dont know how long to replace it but it is alot easier to just sit in the seat than it is to get under the dash and/or the car.||||reg||1
Z||000010||kong||06-06-2000||03:13 PM|||| Tom's right, I tightened mine up in about 25 minutes ( not counting the time it took to go buy the proper socket for it )   the hardest part is compressing the the spring to get the clip back on. Other than that it's a piece of cake.||||reg||1
Z||000011||My7Fieros||10-07-2000||09:31 PM||||Disregard....... see my new post "Gettin' Jiggly with it"....thanx.<p>[This message has been edited by My7Fieros (edited 10-07-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||theogre||10-07-2000||09:57 PM||||The thing won't fall off if that's what you want to know. Wouldn't drive it that way at all myself. I tore mine out and fixed it the day after I got the car.<P>If you have the tools or know someone that does then it's not rocket science to fix. More of a jigsaw. If you aren't careful it can hurt you or you'll break it worse. Can break a finger if you get pinched in the tilt. Can break the tilt if you pry/hit the wrong thing.<P>If you don't have the tools then its easier to just replace the thing. You don't need much for that. Columns are usually not that expensive used. All you need to replace is a set of metric sockets and a wheel puller if you change steering wheels. If it's an automatic you also need a small (#1) Phillips screw driver to get the transmission interlock off the switch.<P>There's a whole bunch of notes about this in my cave. (link on bottom of this page)<P>You don't have to get under the car to work on this. It's all under the dash. 5 bolts/nuts and if automatic 2 small screws. not counting dealing with trim. (4 trim screws in Fiero.)<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 10-07-2000).]||||reg||1
